# 7. Why Does Random Forest Reduce Overfitting? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

A single Decision Tree can have:

```text
Low Bias
High Variance
```

It can become highly dependent on the specific training dataset.

Random Forest averages many trees.

Conceptually:

```text
Individual Tree Errors
        ↓
Different Errors
        ↓
Aggregation
        ↓
Reduced Variance
        ↓
Better Generalization
```

### Important Interview Point 🔥

Random Forest primarily improves over a single Decision Tree by:

> **Reducing variance.**

It does not simply mean "more trees = always better."

The diversity between trees is crucial.

---

# 8. The Mathematics Behind Ensemble Variance

Suppose individual models have variance:

\[
\sigma^2
\]

If models were completely independent, averaging them would significantly reduce variance.

However, in reality trees are correlated.

Conceptually:

\[
\text{Ensemble Quality}
\approx
\text{Individual Tree Strength}
+
\text{Tree Diversity}
\]

The more correlated the trees are, the less benefit averaging provides.

This is exactly why Random Forest uses:

- Bootstrap sampling
- Random feature selection

---

# 9. Out-of-Bag (OOB) Samples ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

When we create a bootstrap sample, some original samples are not selected for that particular tree.

These are called:

\[
\boxed{\text{Out-of-Bag Samples}}
\]

Example:

```text
Original:
A B C D E
```

Tree training sample:

```text
A B B D E
```

Then:

```text
C
```

is Out-of-Bag for that tree.

---

## OOB Evaluation

That unused sample can help evaluate the model.

Conceptually:

```text
Sample C
    ↓
Not used to train Tree 1
    ↓
Use Tree 1 for prediction
```

Across many trees, this provides an internal estimate of generalization performance.

