# 14. Data & Compute Requirements

## Data

Random Forest works particularly well with:

- Tabular datasets
- Nonlinear relationships
- Feature interactions

It can also handle many practical datasets effectively with relatively limited preprocessing.

---

## Feature Scaling

Unlike KNN:

> Random Forest generally does **not** require feature scaling.

Why?

Because trees use threshold-based splits.

Example:

```text
Age > 30
```

Scaling changes the numerical threshold but generally preserves the ordering.

---

## Compute

Compared with one Decision Tree:

```text
Single Tree → Faster
Random Forest → More expensive
```

But individual trees can often be trained independently, making ensemble training amenable to parallel computation.

---

# 15. Trade-Off Analysis

## Advantages

✅ Usually generalizes better than one Decision Tree  
✅ Reduces variance  
✅ Captures nonlinear relationships  
✅ Learns feature interactions  
✅ Less prone to overfitting than a single deep tree  
✅ No feature scaling generally required  
✅ Works for classification and regression  

---

## Disadvantages & Limitations

❌ Less interpretable than one Decision Tree  
❌ More memory usage  
❌ Slower than a single tree  
❌ Can still struggle with certain highly imbalanced problems without appropriate handling  
❌ May not be the best choice for extremely high-dimensional sparse data  
❌ Large forests increase inference cost

